  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 221 PM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nAn upper level low pressure is over Kansas and Nebraska. This has \nresulted in increased upper lift across the region with rain \nshowers. Winds across south central and central Nebraska and north \ncentral Kansas are mostly out of the south to southeast at around 10 \nto 15 MPH with gusts up to around 20 MPH. High temperatures today \nwill range from the mid 50s to mid 60s. Rain showers are expected to \ndecrease in coverage tonight with low temperatures generally ranging \nfrom the upper 40s to mid 50s. Light rain showers and/or drizzle are \nexpected to continue into Sunday with south to southeast winds \ncontinuing across the area. Temperatures on Sunday will be a little \nwarmer than today with a strengthening surface trough to the west. \nRain showers with some thunderstorms will increase across the area \nSunday night as an upper trough to the west moves closer to the \nregion. Low temperatures Sunday night will be warmer than the \nprevious night (lows mainly in the mid 50s to low 60s) as the \nsurface trough to the west expands across the area. \n\nThe upper trough will move over the Central Plains on Monday with a \ncold front moving into the area from the west and northwest. While \nthere is some uncertainty with high temperatures on Monday due to \nthe timing of the front, expect highs to generally range from the \nmid 60s to mid/upper 70s. Showers and thunderstorms will continue \nacross the area on Monday. Storm chances will increase along the \nfront late Monday afternoon into Monday night. Low temperatures \nMonday night are expected to range from the low 40s to low 50s. Rain \nchances (up to around 50% chance) continue on Tuesday with the upper \ntrough/low over the area. There are some model \ndifferences/uncertainty with high temperatures on Tuesday due to \nuncertainty in the strength of the cooler airmass but expect highs \nto mostly be in the 50s. Mostly dry conditions are expected \nWednesday through Friday with high temperatures generally in the 50s \nand 60s. Low temperatures Tuesday night, Wednesday night, Thursday \nnight, and Friday night are expected to be in the 30s and 40s.",
